module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.nested_membership_scan_where_a_map_primitive_exists

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import std.decl_ref { DeclarationRef, WholeDeclaration }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { BlockerClassUnstated, RecurringFailureMode, RungUnread }

data nested_membership_scan_where_a_map_primitive_exists: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "nested_membership_scan_where_a_map_primitive_exists" as NonEmptyStr,

  rung: RungUnread { cause: BlockerClassUnstated },

  receipts: [
    "**the cheap-to-write form of a correct completeness oracle is a nested scan, and nothing makes the map-backed form the default** INVALID STATE: an identity join over two populations is written as `all` over one containing `any` over the other -- quadratic in eval steps -- while `v2.std.collection` `empty_map` / `map_insert` / `map_lookup` are PrimitiveContracts already imported by 78 `dag/` modules. The author is not careless: the nested form is the one the substrate's list vocabulary suggests, and the map primitives live in a folder the author reads as someone else's layer.",

    "HARM, AND IT CHANGED CHARACTER ON 2026-09-13. Before the two-tier ceiling a quadratic join was slow. After it, a NEW witness over the 100ms-equivalent budget REFUSES -- so the cheap-to-write form of a correct oracle is now the form the floor rejects, and the author's first encounter with the map primitives is a red run rather than a review comment.",

    "THE DISTINGUISHING FACT THAT MAKES THIS A CLASS AND NOT AN INCIDENT: the correctness is not in question in any instance. Every specimen so far is a CORRECT join -- both directions, count intact -- written in the shape the vocabulary suggested. So the recognition rule is not 'the oracle is wrong'; it is 'the oracle is right and its cost shape is quadratic in a population that grows'. Reviewing for correctness finds nothing.",

    "SPECIMEN A, the join: gunbc#10994, the first live application of the two-tier ruling. A correct identity join written as two nested all/any scans measured 2n^2 at 618,000 eval steps. Reported by fierce-lark-661, 2026-09-13.",

    "SPECIMEN B, and it is the instructive one because the REPAIR is what failed: gunbc#11195's grandfathered roster, 3,795 identities, measured three ways on this repository's own [witness] line. (1) `any` over a flattened roster: 92,490 eval steps, 148ms. (2) map-backed via empty_map/map_insert/map_lookup: 39,940 eval steps -- THE FEWEST OF THE THREE -- and 36,000ms of CPU. (3) a short-circuiting descent over chunks that never flattens: 8,066 steps / 19ms on a hit, 43,583 / 76ms on a miss.",

    "WHY SPECIMEN B IS A SECOND DEFECT AND NOT A CAVEAT ON THE FIRST. `map_insert` returns a NEW map, so folding n rows in is O(n^2) HOST work, and none of it is an eval step because it happens inside a primitive. The prescribed repair for the nested scan therefore reads as the CHEAPEST spelling on the clock the floor gates -- 39,940 against a 72,300 budget -- while costing 400x the CPU and breaching the 8,000ms wall deadline. THIS IS NOT GUIDANCE FOR AUTHORS. A per-site rule for choosing scan-vs-map by n prices an exception around a cost-shape defect, which DESIGN section 6 forbids in terms; the defect is the map's REALIZATION, which has no bulk constructor, so the idiom can only be expressed as an insert fold.",

    "REPAIR, AND IT IS ONE REPAIR IN THE PRIMITIVE RATHER THAN A RULE IN EVERY CONSUMER: `v2.std.collection` gains a bulk constructor -- `map_from_list` / `map_from_pairs` as a single primitive call, O(n) or O(n log n) host -- or a persistent structure with structural sharing so insert is O(log n). The identity-join idiom then uses the bulk form and the nested scan has a default worth reaching for. Owner: the primitive's lane (clever-fox-89, gunbc#11121, primitive-backed map delegates); briefed by fierce-lark-661.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable. Both specimens were caught by a human reading a diff or by an author measuring on a hunch; nothing refuses a nested join, and the new-witness tier refuses it only once it is over budget, which is a consequence rather than a detector -- it cannot tell a quadratic join from an honestly expensive witness, and it says nothing at all about the map-backed form, which passes the step clock while costing more.",

    "ATTAINABLE CEILING: mechanically preventable. The shape is a source-level pattern over the Node tree -- a fold over one population containing a membership fold over another -- and the `complexity_accumulator_copy` lens family already prices folds of exactly this kind. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, naming the CAPABILITY: that lens recognises the nested-membership shape and refuses it on the acceptance path, sufficient that a quadratic join cannot land whether or not it happens to exceed a budget on the day it lands. A trigger that named the two-tier ceiling instead would be satisfied while the class stayed alive, because the ceiling only sees the instances that cross it.",

    "SPECIMEN WHERE THE MAP WAS ALREADY BUILT AND CARRIED (2026-10-15). v2.compiler.name_resolve validate_module_roots derives every root's module name once and keeps it on the resolution context as ValidatedModuleRoots.by_name, refusing a duplicate or unreadable name for the whole context; subject_module_root_for_name already read it. But the import admissions -- admit_import_visible_entry (ImportScoped) and admit_imported_module_existence (NamespaceOnlyY, added by gunbc#14072) -- were handed the raw root list and went through module_root_find, whose module_root_lookup folds over every root re-deriving qualified_name_from_module_node. Once gunbc#14029 made the closure build every module's namespace, the cost was modules x imports x modules name derivations: the whole-tree //gunbc/instruments:v2-native-census ran past four hours at main 2f87f93d8f, perf putting about ninety percent of the run under module_root_lookup, and thrashed at its memory cap. The native-seven closure (139 modules) hid it. The repair routes every post-validation admission through the one map read (validated_module_root_for_name) and keeps the scan only for roots nobody validated (v2.compiler.self_host.frontier_probe). Discriminating evidence: v2.test.claim.name_resolve.admission_fail_closed admission_reads_the_validated_map_not_the_root_list supplies a validated set whose LIST holds two roots of one name and whose MAP holds one, so a rescan refuses as a duplicate while a map read admits; reverting either admission to the scan turns it red. The class's tell here is narrower than a nested any/all: a consumer of a carrier that already holds the keyed fact re-derives it from the population the carrier was built from.",

    "THE SAME CLASS ONE LINK OVER, IN THE SAME RUN. With the module-root scan gone, the profile moved to v2.std.cross_tree.resolution source_root_index_lookup (78-87% of resolve): SourceRootIndex was an association list folded per question, and the cross-tree gate asks it twice per import of every module. It is now a map from module to its SourceRootLookup, the ambiguity rule decided once at insert (v2.test.claim.cross_tree_source_root_index). And at ingest the census position table held one persistent-map entry per occurrence id of the whole tree to answer which file owns an id; ids come from one allocator threaded through the files, so the answer is one range per accepted file found by a binary search (v2.std.sorted_floor, shared with v2.std.source_position; v2.test.claim.provenance.occurrence_file_attribution census_owner_ranges_attribute_each_root_to_its_own_file_holds). Receipt, //gunbc/instruments:v2-native-census over the whole tree: at 2f87f93d8f it ran past four hours and was killed thrashing at a 56 GB cap; with the three repairs it completes in 78 minutes wall (about 20 of them emitting and building the census compiler) at a 42.6 GB peak, 8490 modules.",

    "THE MEMBERSHIP QUERY THAT REBUILT ITS OWN UNIVERSE PER QUESTION (2026-10-20). The tell in its purest form: dag_lex_token_symbol(sym) and dag_lex_keyword_symbol(sym) (v2.extdeps.languages.dag) answered 'is sym a token-class rule?' by calling lex_rule_set_token_class_member / lex_rule_set_keyword_class_member over a FRESH dag_lex_rules() -- every query rebuilt the whole rule set (dag_lex_rules_except_braces, the brace and hole-mode sets, dag_literal_lex_rule with its unicode_scalar_unfold over each literal row) and then scanned it linearly. The rule set is a module-literal constant: the rebuild computed the same map every time, per query. The fold path asked at body-lowering frequency -- v2.compiler.body_lowering_fold body_lower_record_literal_tag_optional and body_lower_call_callee_read call the queries once per record-literal head and once per call operand. MEASURED, on a fold-heavy DAG corpus (a module of record literals with named fields plus named-argument calls, built to an exact byte layout and replicated 1x/2x/4x at about 15/27/53 KB; the corpus builder, the pipeline rungs tokenize_prepared -> parse_module_prepared -> normalize, and the counting probe live on branch scratch/nfl-measure-probe at c5559811ca2, run with GUNBC_EVAL_MEMO=0 -- the memo's hit rate falls with input size, so memo-on before/after counts are not comparable), per pipeline, BEFORE -> AFTER: lex_rule_token_class_member 134,400/268,800/537,600 -> 0; lex_rule_set_token_class_member 768/1,536/3,072 -> 0; unicode_scalar_unfold 117,282/234,403/468,645 -> 698/1,083/1,853 (the remainder is the parse of the corpus text itself, the query contributed 466,792 of the 4x's 468,645); dag_literal_lex_rule 68,441/136,793/273,497 -> 178; dag_lex_rules_except_braces 2,307/4,611/9,219 -> 6; dag_lex_rules 769/1,537/3,073 -> 2 (the measured total; the counter does not attribute the two calls -- dag_prepared_lex also builds the lexer from dag_lex_rules(), so the two builds are not claimable one-per-data, only the collapse from thousands per query to a constant is); the QUERIES unchanged -- dag_lex_token_symbol 768/1,536/3,072 and the callers body_lower_record_literal_tag_optional 864/1,728/3,456, body_lower_call_callee_read 288/576/1,152 on both sides: the work below the queries moved, the questions did not. THE REPAIR carries the first value rather than caching a later request (DESIGN section 2): lex_rule_set_token_class_map already existed in v2.std.compilers.lexing as a single fold, so the token map and a new keyword twin (lex_rule_set_keyword_class_map, gated on lex_pattern_is_keyword -- the same authority the deleted linear member consulted) are bound ONCE as data dag_lex_token_class_membership / dag_lex_keyword_class_membership beside the prepared lexer, and the readers answer by map_lookup. ROOT GENERALIZATION (§6b), answered in this repair rather than deferred: the same per-query rebuild sat in EVERY language model that queried membership -- ecmascript_lex_token_symbol, go_lex_token_symbol, java_canonical_symbols, kotlin_lex_token_symbol, lean_lex_token_symbol, python_lex_token_symbol, swift_lex_token_symbol, ts_lex_token_symbol, wasm_lex_token_symbol, and rust twice (rust_fn_add_lex_token_symbol over rust_fn_add_lex_rules, rust_canonical_symbols over rust_lex_rules). Each now carries its own *_lex_token_class_membership data built by the one fold and answers via std.algebra map_contains_key; the two manual grammar claims (java_grammar_structure, rust_grammar_structure) build a claim-local membership data the same way. With every consumer rewired, BOTH linear member pairs are deleted from v2.std.compilers.lexing -- lex_rule_keyword_class_member / lex_rule_set_keyword_class_member and lex_rule_token_class_member / lex_rule_set_token_class_member -- because a re-derivation of a fact its carrier already holds has no reason to remain once no consumer asks for it (delete-first, §3); the map fold and lex_rule_token_class_optional stay as the carried construction. The equivalence is structural, not hoped: for any symbol, the map answers Present exactly when the old fold-OR found a visible rule whose token_class equals it -- both run over lex_rule_set_matching_rules, the map keys on the same rule.token_class the scan compared. EVIDENCE, WHAT IT DOES AND DOES NOT PROVE: the acceptance this class owes a rewrite of a correctness-bearing lookup is that the lookups answer the same -- and that is what was checked. The normalized ROOT of every rung digests the same before and after -- Fnv1a64Structural 27a0c020b8bd3e14 at 1x, ececee09730767c0 at 2x, 6d2dedae262f18cd at 4x -- and the same rungs re-digest identically after the root generalization touched the ten sibling modules. This is a MATCHING NORMALIZED-ROOT STRUCTURAL DIGEST at all three sizes, NOT byte-identical normalize output: content_hash folds node kinds, edge labels and child hashes but canonicalizes occurrence identity away (v2.std.node canonicalize_node_for_content_hash stamps every node OccurrenceSynthetic), and NormalizedTree carries import_bindings, test_markers, type_declaration_kinds/modifiers, imported_modules and member_declarations BESIDE root, none of which the probe compares. Two normalized trees can differ in those carried channels and share a root digest. The scratch probe at its first commit (scratch/nfl-measure-probe @ c5559811ca2) worded this as byte-identity; it overstated, and the probe's own wording is corrected at d215241304e on that branch. WHY IT IS A MEMBER OF THIS ROW AND NOT A NEW ONE: the class's tell stated above -- a consumer of a carrier that already holds the keyed fact re-derives it from the population the carrier was built from -- and here the carrier WAS BUILT PER CONSUMER, so the re-derivation was the carrier's own construction; the map primitive already existed for every other consumer, which is what makes it authored duplication rather than a missing primitive. The v1 route also answers per-InterpContext (v1_interpreter data_cache, keyed at the declaration node, not gated by the memo switch) and the native route once per accessing thread (the emitter's non-Copy call-body data realizes as a thread_local! once cell -- it initializes the first time EACH thread touches it, not once process-wide), so in every realization the first value is carried rather than re-derived per query.",

    "WHAT THIS ROW DOES NOT CLAIM. It does not claim the map primitives are the wrong answer -- they are the right shape and the wrong realization, which is why the repair is filed against the realization. It does not claim a number for the n at which the insert fold becomes the more expensive choice; that number is a property of a defect that is scheduled to be removed, and pinning it would give the workaround a specification.",
  ],

  evidence: [
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.algebra", decl_name: "map_insert", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "std.algebra", decl_name: "map_lookup", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.floor_grandfathered_roster", decl_name: "floor_grandfathered_holds", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "v2.workflow.required_floor", decl_name: "new_witness_over_budget_first_remedy", field: WholeDeclaration },
    DeclarationRef { module_path: "gunbc.rung_drop.floor_cost_cpu_regression_at_constant_eval_steps", decl_name: "floor_cost_cpu_regression_at_constant_eval_steps", field: WholeDeclaration },
  ],
}
